From 68c705dd830fcb4f13c16c4a1b4a9626b82bc607 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 00:04:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] nvkms, nvidia-drm: NV_DDC_I2C_PORT and NV_OUTPUT_LINK,
 read-only

A client that wants to know whether a display that gave a hot-plug pulse
lost the scrambling setup of its link (SCDC TMDS_Config, which the source
writes only when it sets a head up: in NVIDIA's case the closed part of the
driver) had no way to tell which I2C bus is the DDC of a connector, nor
whether the link of its head is scrambled at all. It could only guess from
the mode.

Two read-only connector properties of nvidia-drm, from two Hades dpy
attributes of NVKMS through KAPI (getDisplayDdcI2cPort(),
getDisplayOutputLink()):

- NV_DDC_I2C_PORT (0..255): the I2C port of the DDC of the connector's
  display, ddcPortId - 1 of NV0073_CTRL_CMD_SPECIFIC_GET_I2C_PORTID (ddcPortId
  counts from 1, 0 for none). That is the index under which the nvidia module
  registers the adapter of the port, "NVIDIA i2c adapter <index> at
  <bus>:<slot>.<fn>": one for each implemented and valid port, index = port,
  and the displays of DisplayPort are added to the one of ddcPortId - 1
  (RmI2cAddGpuPorts(), rm_i2c_add_adapter(), nv_i2c_add_adapter()). Whether
  the module registered that one (it keeps MAX_I2C_ADAPTERS) shows in the
  name of the adapter. Set when a display is detected on the connector, and
  kept when it goes; 255 before that, for no port, or when RM does not say.
- NV_OUTPUT_LINK (0..0xffffffff): how NVKMS drives the connector's display,
  set where NV_OUTPUT_BPC is (after a commit that sets the head again); 0
  while it drives nothing or when NVKMS does not say. Bits 0-7: 1 TMDS not
  scrambled, 2 TMDS scrambled (a TMDS character rate above 340 MHz: the
  pixel clock times bpc / 8 for RGB and YCbCr 4:4:4, the pixel clock for
  4:2:2, half of it times bpc / 8 for the 4:2:0 of the hardware), 3 HDMI FRL,
  4 DisplayPort. Bits 8-15: the HDMI_FRL_DATA_RATE of FRL (1..6), 0
  otherwise. Bits 16-23: the pixel format, 0 RGB, 1 YCbCr 4:4:4, 2 YCbCr
  4:2:2, 3 YCbCr 4:2:0.

A link at 340 MHz or less goes as 1 even where the display declares
scrambling at those rates: whether RM enables it there is not known from the
open part of the driver. Nothing that goes out changes.

Made again for 620.70.06: the code around it changed there; the change itself is
the same, with the names and types of that release.
